        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Silica sand works between pavers when the joints are tight, the surface is pedestrian and you are happy to top it up; it is the wrong choice under the pavers and a poor one where weeds, ants or washout are the problem. The width of your joints decides more than the brand on the bag.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What 'Silica Sand' Actually Means on a Bag
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost all sand is silica sand. Quartz, which is crystalline silica, is the most common mineral in ordinary sand, and OSHA's own description of crystalline silica lists sand alongside stone, concrete and mortar as materials that contain it. So when a bag in the hardscape aisle says silica sand, it is not telling you much about the mineral. What it is really describing is the processing: a sand that has been washed, dried and screened to a fairly fine, consistent size.
          </p>

          <p>
            That processing is what matters for paver joints. Joint sand has to flow down into gaps that may be an eighth of an inch wide or less, and it can only do that if it is dry and fine. A damp sand clumps and bridges across the top of the joint, leaving a hollow underneath that collapses the first time it rains. A sand with large particles in it wedges at the top for the same reason. A bagged, dried silica sand avoids both problems, which is why it was the standard paver joint filler for decades before polymeric sand took over most residential work.
          </p>

          <p>
            It helps to know that the products competing with it are made of the same stuff. Polymeric sand is mostly graded sand with a binder blended through it; Alliance, for example, states that its Gator polymeric sands follow an ASTM C144 gradation, the same gradation standard used for fine masonry sand. Play sand is silica sand too. The difference between play sand and a good joint sand is grain shape, size distribution and how the sand was processed, which is the whole subject of our comparison of <a href="/paver-sand-vs-play-sand/">paver sand vs play sand</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            So the useful question is not whether to use silica sand, but which grade of sand goes in which part of the job, and whether a joint of plain sand is good enough for your patio or whether it needs a binder. The rest of this guide works through those questions in the order you will meet them on site.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="where-it-goes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Which Sand Goes Where: Bedding vs Joints
          </h2>

          <p>
            A sand-set paver surface uses sand in two places, and they need opposite properties. We cover the full detail in <a href="/what-is-paver-sand/">what paver sand is</a>, so this is the short version with one question in mind: where does a fine silica sand fit, and where does it not?
          </p>

          <p>
            Under the pavers, in the bedding layer, fine silica sand does not belong. The bedding layer is an inch or so of coarse, washed concrete sand conforming to ASTM C33, screeded flat over a compacted gravel base. The Brick Industry Association's technical note on clay pavers and Pine Hall Brick's technical bulletin on sand both specify C33 concrete sand for bedding, and Pine Hall specifically warns that fine mason's sand, the ASTM C144 grade, settles unevenly when used as bedding. A fine sand under the pavers behaves like a cushion rather than a seat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between the pavers, the picture flips. Industry guidance accepts either coarse C33 sand or finer C144 sand in the joints, and the choice is a trade-off. Pine Hall's bulletin explains it plainly: C33 compacts better and lets you use one sand for the whole job, but some of its particles are too big to fit a joint of about an eighth of an inch and wedge at the top. Finer C144 sand gets into narrow joints easily, but, in the bulletin's words, the easier a sand goes into the joints, the easier it will come out. That is the core weakness of any fine joint sand, and it is why Pine Hall advises against specifying finer joint sands for vehicular paving.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Where</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Right sand</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Fine silica sand?</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bedding layer (about 1 inch)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Washed concrete sand, ASTM C33</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No; settles unevenly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Narrow joints, patios and paths</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine dry joint sand (C144) or polymeric</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Yes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wider joints, 1/2 inch and up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polymeric sand or stone dust products rated for the width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Washes out quickly</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Driveways</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">C33 or polymeric rated for vehicles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Not recommended</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Permeable paver systems</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">The open-graded stone the system specifies</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">No</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            One more place sand turns up is on top of the finished surface, swept over the pavers so it can be vibrated down into the joints. That is a technique rather than a third material, and it has its own section below, because what you do with the leftover sand on top depends entirely on whether it is plain or polymeric.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="between-pavers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Can You Use Silica Sand Between Pavers?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Yes, and for some surfaces it is the better choice. Plain joint sand has three genuine advantages over polymeric sand. It never cracks, because there is nothing in it to crack. It never hazes the paver faces, because there is no binder to wash onto them. And it can be topped up in five minutes with a broom, with no watering sequence, no rain window and no risk of a botched installation that has to be ground out.
          </p>

          <p>
            Those advantages matter most on a garden path or a small patio with tight joints, in a shaded spot where moss would grow on polymeric joints anyway, on pavers you expect to lift for utility access, and on old brick patios that were built with sand joints and have worked happily for years. Pine Hall's bulletin also notes that sand joints naturally seal over time as fine silt and debris settle into the top of the joint, which is why a well-established sand-jointed patio often behaves better than a new one.
          </p>

          <p>
            The weaknesses are the familiar ones. Plain sand washes out in heavy rain and on slopes, it gets blown out by leaf blowers and blasted out by pressure washers, weeds germinate in it, and ants excavate it. If any of those is the reason you are re-sanding in the first place, plain sand will put you back in the same position in a season or two. That is the territory where polymeric sand earns its price, and our comparison of <a href="/polymeric-sand-vs-regular-sand-for-pavers/">polymeric sand vs regular sand</a> sets out the costs and lifespans side by side.
          </p>

          <p>
            One thing not to do is mix the two in the same joint. Alliance's FAQ calls the practice of half-filling a joint with one material and finishing it with polymeric sand topping, says it can cause the joint to fail, and states that partially filling a joint with regular sand voids its warranty. If you are switching an existing patio from plain sand to polymeric, the old sand has to come out to depth first, as covered in our guide to <a href="/can-you-apply-polymeric-sand-over-existing-sand/">applying polymeric sand over existing sand</a>.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Plain sand for tight, low-stress joints. Polymeric where something keeps emptying them.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">If your joints are narrow, the surface is foot traffic only and you do not mind sweeping more sand in each spring, a fine dry silica sand is cheap and forgiving. If rain, ants, weeds or a pressure washer keep emptying the joints, step up to a polymeric sand rated for your joint width.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="brick-patios" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Brick Patio Joints: Why Width Decides the Sand
          </h2>

          <p>
            Clay brick patios deserve their own section, because they are where the best sand for the joints is most often different from the best sand for a concrete paver patio. Clay pavers are commonly laid with very tight joints, and many older brick patios were laid almost hand-tight with only a hairline of sand between the units. That tightness is what drives the sand choice.
          </p>

          <p>
            Start by measuring. Look at a dozen joints across the patio, not just the one by the door, and size them against something you know: a 1/8-inch drill bit either slides into a joint or it does not. If most joints will not take the bit, you are below an eighth of an inch, and that matters because most polymeric sands set a minimum. Alliance, for example, lists a minimum joint width of 1/8 inch for both Gator Maxx G2 and Gator Supersand Bond G2. Below that, a fine, dry silica sand is often the only material that will actually reach the bottom of the joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the joints are an eighth of an inch or wider and you want the benefits of a binder, polymeric sand works on clay pavers, but clay needs extra care. Alliance's product pages say clay pavers must be completely dry before installation and its FAQ warns that clay can still hold moisture when it looks dry; moisture is what triggers early activation and haze. Pine Hall Brick's bulletin makes the same point about haze and names two products it has found less prone to hazing on clay pavers: Techniseal HP NextGel and SEK Surebond PolySweep. Whatever you buy, do a test area first.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the brick patio is mortared, none of this applies. A mortar-set brick patio sits on a concrete slab with mortar joints, and sweeping sand into cracked mortar joints is a cosmetic patch that will wash out. Those joints need repointing with mortar, not sand of any kind.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Joint width</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best joint material</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notes</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Under 1/8 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine, dry silica sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Below the minimum most polymeric sands list</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/8 to 1/2 inch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fine joint sand or polymeric</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polymeric if weeds, ants or washout are a problem</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1/2 inch and up</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Polymeric rated for the width</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Plain sand washes out quickly at this width</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Mortared brick</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mortar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sand is not a repair for mortar joints</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="sand-on-top" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sweeping Sand on Top of Pavers: How Much and How Long
          </h2>

          <p>
            Joint sand goes in from the top. You spread it over the finished surface, sweep it across the joints, and vibrate it down with a plate compactor, repeating until the joints are full. How that goes, and what you do with the sand left on top afterwards, is where plain and polymeric sand part company.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The method for either sand</h3>

          <p>
            The surface and the sand must both be dry. Tip a few small piles across the area, then sweep diagonally across the joint lines with a stiff push broom, so the sand drops into the joints instead of being pushed along them. Run a plate compactor fitted with a protective pad over the surface to settle the sand and the pavers, then sweep in more sand and compact again. Two or three rounds is normal; the joints are full when sand stops disappearing into them. Alliance's FAQ is blunt about why compaction matters, saying that uncompacted joints can be missing 30 to 50 per cent of the sand they need.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plain silica sand: leaving a little on top</h3>

          <p>
            With plain sand, many installers leave a thin dusting on the surface for a few days so that foot traffic and the first light rain work more of it into the joints, then sweep the excess off. That is a reasonable approach on a new installation, because joint sand keeps settling for a while. The trade-offs are that loose sand gets tracked indoors, it is gritty underfoot, and it should never be left on a surface you are about to seal, because the sealer will glue it to the paver faces.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Polymeric sand: nothing on top, ever</h3>

          <p>
            With polymeric sand, every grain left on the paver face before watering becomes a speck of binder stuck to the surface, and enough of them become haze. Sweep the faces clean and blow them off with a leaf blower on a low setting held at a shallow angle. Alliance's G2 product pages say no blower is needed, but its general FAQ still recommends blowing off dust from several directions, and the conservative approach costs five minutes. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-use-polymeric-sand/">how to use polymeric sand</a> covers the full watering sequence.
          </p>

          <p>
            Topping up an established plain-sand patio is the same job in miniature: wait for a dry spell, sweep dry sand into any joints that have dropped, mist lightly if you want to settle it, and sweep the excess away. Once a year is typical for a sheltered patio, more often near downspouts, slopes and anywhere you use a pressure washer. If the sand keeps vanishing faster than that, our guide to <a href="/how-to-keep-sand-between-pavers/">keeping sand between pavers</a> works through the causes in order.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="silica-dust" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Silica Dust: Sweeping, Cutting and What OSHA Says
          </h2>

          <p>
            Because sand is mostly crystalline silica, it is worth separating the real hazard from the imagined one. OSHA describes respirable crystalline silica as very small particles, at least 100 times smaller than ordinary sand you might find on beaches and playgrounds, and lists the diseases linked to breathing it: silicosis, lung cancer, chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and kidney disease. The sand grains you sweep into joints are not respirable. The fine dust that comes with them, and especially the dust made by cutting, is.
          </p>

          <p>
            OSHA states that respirable crystalline silica is created when cutting, sawing, grinding, drilling and crushing stone, rock, concrete, brick, block and mortar. On a paver job that means cutting pavers is by far the largest exposure. OSHA's construction standard for silica, 29 CFR 1926.1153, sets a permissible exposure limit of 50 micrograms per cubic metre averaged over an eight-hour day, and for handheld power saws its Table 1 specifies a saw equipped with an integrated water delivery system that continuously feeds water to the blade. Used outdoors for four hours or less a shift, that control needs no respirator under the table; over four hours, or in an enclosed area at any duration, it calls for a respirator with an assigned protection factor of 10.
          </p>

          <p>
            The same standard addresses sweeping directly. Its housekeeping rule says employers shall not allow dry sweeping or dry brushing where such activity could contribute to exposure, and points to wet sweeping, HEPA-filtered vacuuming or other low-exposure methods instead. That rule is written for employers, not homeowners, and sweeping clean bagged sand into joints is a much smaller source of dust than sweeping up cutting debris. But it is the best benchmark available, and the takeaway is clear: the dust to worry about is the fine grey powder left after cutting, not the sand itself.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice, for a DIY patio, that means a few habits. Cut pavers wet, with a wet saw or a water-fed cut-off saw, or use a paver splitter, which makes no dust at all. Clean up cutting slurry and dust wet or with a vacuum fitted with a HEPA filter, rather than dry-sweeping it. Wear a well-fitted N95 or better respirator when pouring bags, sweeping dusty material or cutting, keep children and pets away while you work, and stand upwind. Polymeric sand bags carry the same silica warnings on their safety data sheets, and Alliance describes its Gator sands as compliant with the OSHA silica standard, but the dust you make with a saw has nothing to do with what is in the bag.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">The OSHA Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">50 µg/m³</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Permissible exposure limit, 8-hour average</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">25 µg/m³</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Action level</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">100x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Respirable silica is at least this much smaller than ordinary sand</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">APF 10</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Respirator class for long or enclosed saw work</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Buying Joint Sand: What to Look For on the Bag
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most bags in the hardscape aisle will tell you enough to choose, as long as you know which words matter. For plain joint sand the first is dry. Kiln-dried or bagged dry sand flows into joints; damp bulk sand from a yard pile does not, and drying it yourself on a tarp is slower than it sounds. The second is the gradation. A bag that cites ASTM C144 is a fine masonry-grade sand suited to narrow joints; ASTM C33 is coarser concrete sand, ideal for bedding and acceptable in wider joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            What to avoid is just as specific. Play sand and beach sand are rounded and will not lock up, which is why they fail under and between pavers alike. Stone dust and screenings hold water, and Alliance's FAQ notes that polymeric sand fails over a stone dust base for exactly that reason. Bags labelled as all-purpose sand vary widely, so look for a stated gradation before you buy several of them.
          </p>

          <p>
            For quantity, joint sand is a small part of the job. Narrow joints take far less than wide ones, and the coverage on a bag of plain sand depends on joint width and paver thickness just as it does for polymeric. Our <a href="/sand-calculator/">paver sand calculator</a> works out bedding sand and joint sand from your dimensions, and buying one bag more than it suggests saves an evening trip to the store.
          </p>

          <p>
            If, after all this, polymeric sand is the right answer for your joints, choose it by joint width and base type rather than by brand. Our ranking of the <a href="/best-polymeric-sand/">best polymeric sand</a> compares the main options, and our <a href="/alliance-gator-polymeric-sand-review/">Alliance Gator polymeric sand review</a> decodes one maker's lineup, from narrow-joint bags to polymeric stone dust for flagstone. Plain silica sand remains the right call for tight, low-stress joints; it just is not the right call everywhere.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using Fine Sand as Bedding</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Fine silica or mason's sand under the pavers settles unevenly and turns the bedding layer into a cushion. The bedding layer needs coarse, washed ASTM C33 concrete sand, screeded about an inch deep over compacted gravel. Keep the fine sand for the joints, where its ability to flow into narrow gaps is an advantage.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sweeping Damp Sand Into Joints</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Damp sand clumps and bridges across the top of the joint, leaving a hollow underneath that collapses in the first rain. Use bagged dry sand, wait for a dry surface, and sweep diagonally across the joints so the grains drop all the way down rather than skating along the top.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Sand on the Surface Before Sealing</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Any loose sand on the paver faces when a sealer goes on gets glued to the surface as a gritty, permanent texture. Sweep and blow the pavers completely clean before sealing, and give plain-sand joints time to settle and be topped up first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dry-Cutting Pavers</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Cutting brick and concrete creates respirable crystalline silica, the dust OSHA links to silicosis and lung cancer. Cut wet with a water-fed saw or use a splitter, clean up the slurry wet or with a HEPA vacuum, and wear a well-fitted respirator rather than dry-sweeping the grey dust afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Topping Old Sand With Polymeric</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Filling the bottom of a joint with plain sand and finishing it with polymeric leaves a thin crust over a loose base that breaks up. Alliance says the practice can cause failure and voids its warranty. Remove old sand to the depth the bag specifies before switching materials.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you use silica sand between pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Fine, dry silica sand is a traditional joint filler and still a good choice for tight joints on patios and paths with foot traffic only. It never cracks or hazes and is easy to top up. Its weaknesses are washout, weeds and ants, so where those are the problem, a polymeric sand rated for your joint width is the better choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best sand for brick patio joints?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on joint width. For clay brick laid nearly tight, with joints under about an eighth of an inch, a fine dry silica sand is often the only material that will fill the joint. From an eighth of an inch up, a polymeric sand rated for clay pavers works, provided the brick is completely dry and you test a small area first.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you leave sand on top of pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                With plain joint sand, leaving a thin layer for a few days lets traffic and light rain work more into the joints; sweep the excess off afterwards and never leave it before sealing. With polymeric sand, never. Any sand left on the paver faces before watering can bond to the surface and cause haze.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is silica sand the same as polymeric sand?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, although polymeric sand is mostly silica sand. Polymeric sand has a binder blended through it that activates with water and holds the joint together. Plain silica sand has no binder, so it stays loose and permeable, can be topped up any time, and is more easily washed out, blown out or dug out by ants.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is silica sand dangerous to use?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The sand grains themselves are too large to inhale deeply. The hazard is fine respirable dust, which OSHA says is created mainly by cutting, sawing and grinding brick, stone and concrete. Cut pavers wet or with a splitter, avoid dry-sweeping cutting dust, and wear a well-fitted N95 or better when pouring bags or cutting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is play sand the same as silica sand for pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Play sand is silica sand, but it is the wrong kind for paving. It has been washed and tumbled until its grains are rounded and uniform, so they roll past each other instead of locking together. In joints it washes out quickly, and as bedding it lets pavers shift. Use a fine graded joint sand instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often do you need to top up sand between pavers?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                On a sheltered patio with plain sand joints, about once a year is typical, usually in spring. Joints near downspouts, on slopes, or on surfaces you pressure wash or blow off regularly need topping up more often. If you are re-sanding every few months, polymeric sand is likely the better long-term choice.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

