        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Silica sand is not a special material; it is ordinary quartz sand, dried and screened fine enough to flow into narrow gaps. That makes it a good joint filler for tight, foot-traffic joints, especially on older clay brick patios, and the wrong material for the bedding layer underneath.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Measure your joints before you buy anything. Under an eighth of an inch, fine dry sand is usually the answer; wider than that, or wherever rain, weeds and ants keep emptying the joints, a polymeric sand rated for the width will last longer. Either way, cut pavers wet and leave the dry-swept dust to the sand, not the saw.
          </p>
        </div>

